                  "id": "https://www.thebiotek.com/tev_protease",
                  "title": "TEV",
                  "description": "Structure and Catalytic Mechanism\nTEV protease belongs to the chymotrypsin superfamily (PA clan, C4 family) and features:\n\n\nStructural composition: Two β-barrel domains and a flexible C-terminal tail  .\n\n\nCatalytic triad: Asp-His-Cys, with cysteine acting as the nucleophile instead of serine  .\n\n\nSubstrate binding: Forms an antiparallel β-sheet interaction with the cleavage site, creating a binding tunnel .\n\n\nComparative studies using multiscale modeling reveal that TEV protease combines features of serine proteases (structural fold) and cysteine proteases (nucleophile identity). Substrate binding induces a shift toward protonated cysteine states, enabling catalysis .\nRecognition Sequence and Cleavage Specificity\nThe canonical recognition sequence is ENLYFQ▼(S/G/A/M/C/H), with cleavage occurring between glutamine (Q) and the downstream residue  . Key characteristics include:\nFeatureDetailsP1’ positionTolerates Ser, Gly, Ala, Met, Cys, or His  .Catalytic efficiencyHighest for ENLYFQ▼S (kcat/KM=1.4×104 M−1s−1k_{cat}/K_M = 1.4 \\times 10^4 \\, \\text{M}^{-1}\\text{s}^{-1}kcat/KM=1.4×104M−1s−1) .Temperature rangeActive at 4–34°C; activity declines sharply above 34°C  .\nApplications in Biotechnology\nTEV protease is primarily used for:\n\n\nFusion tag removal: Cleaves affinity tags (e.g., His-tag, GST) from recombinant proteins   .\n\n\nProtein engineering: Facilitates domain shuffling and structural studies  .\n\n\nIn vivo processing: Low toxicity due to rare natural occurrence of its recognition sequence .\n\n\nLimitations and Engineering Solutions\nDespite its utility, TEV protease has inherent challenges addressed through mutagenesis:\nKey Mutations and Their Effects\nMutation(s)ImpactSourceS219VEliminates autolysis by blocking internal cleavage site   .   T17S/N68D/I77VEnhances solubility and stability via surface loop modifications .  C19S/C110S/C130SImproves oxidative stability for disulfide-rich protein applications . Fusion with MBP/SEPIncreases soluble yield (e.g., SEP tag boosts yield by 6.5x)  .  \n\nDirected Evolution and Substrate Engineering\n\n\nAltered specificity: Variants like TEV-PE10 and TEV-PH21 exhibit 5,000-fold preference for Glu or His at the P1 position, enabling tailored cleavage .\n\n\nOxidative stability: Combining C19S/C110S/C130S with solubility-enhancing mutations enables activity in disulfide-refolding buffers .\n\n\nMechanistic Insights\n\n\nDynamic protonation: Substrate binding shifts cysteine between protonated (active) and deprotonated states, with only 13.2% of Michaelis complexes primed for catalysis .\n\n\nThermostability: Engineered variants retain activity at 60°C for extended periods, unlike wild-type .\n\n\nPerformance Metrics of Engineered Variants\nVariantSolubility (mg/L)Activity (kcat/KMk_{cat}/K_Mkcat/KM)Key ApplicationWild-type TEV1.5–21.4×1041.4 \\times 10^41.4×104Standard cleavageT17S/N68D/I77V+S219V102.8×1042.8 \\times 10^42.8×104High-yield purificationTEV-C9R (SEP tag)131.2×1041.2 \\times 10^41.2×104Thermally stable assays\nIndustrial and Research Utility\nTEV protease’s adaptability is highlighted in:\n\n\nDisulfide-rich protein refolding: Oxidative-stable mutants enable tag removal in redox buffers .\n\n\nCryo-EM studies: High-purity variants minimize aggregation during sample preparation .\n\n\nPlant virology: Insights into TEV’s natural role inform antiviral strategies .",
